//? Title: Naive Bayes: Classifying with Independent Evidence
//? Subtitle: Bayes' theorem with conditionally independent Gaussian and smoothed categorical predictors, derived, checked by hand, tested where its assumption holds and where it fails, and applied to the Palmer penguins, using datamunge's stats::NaiveBayesClassifier
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
How should one classify an object from several measurements, some numeric and some categorical,
when there are too few data to model how they vary together? Naive Bayes assumes that the
measurements are independent within each class. Each class is then described by one Gaussian per
numeric predictor and one smoothed frequency table per categorical predictor, and Bayes' theorem
combines them. This report derives the model, its decision boundaries and an exact leave-one-out
estimate of its accuracy. It works a five-row example by hand, including the zero-frequency
problem that Laplace smoothing solves, and a one-dimensional example whose boundary is quadratic
with two roots. Simulations confirm that naive Bayes approaches the Bayes error when its assumption
holds. They also show the two ways it fails: copied predictors make its probabilities overconfident
and move its threshold, and correlated predictors leave its error at the level of a single
predictor while linear discriminant analysis follows the Bayes error. On the Palmer penguins, six
predictors give 97.9% leave-one-out accuracy against 99.4% for linear discriminant analysis, which
models the within-species correlation of the bill measurements. Every accuracy agrees exactly with
scikit-learn.
)

\toc

> The problem

We want to assign an object to one of \(K\) classes from a mixture of measurements: some numeric
(a penguin's bill length, its body mass), some categorical (the island where it was seen, its
sex). The optimal rule is known: predict the class with the largest posterior probability
\(P(c\mid x)\propto\pi_c\,p(x\mid c)\). But it needs the joint density of all the measurements within each
class. With \(p\) numeric predictors a full Gaussian needs \(p(p+3)/2\) parameters per class; a table
over \(p\) categorical predictors with \(L\) levels each needs \(L^p-1\) probabilities per class.
Few data sets can estimate those, and mixing the two kinds needs a joint model of both.

Naive Bayes makes one strong assumption: given the class, the predictors are independent. Then
the joint density is a product of one-dimensional pieces, each estimated on its own: \(2p\)
parameters for Gaussian predictors and \(p(L-1)\) probabilities for categorical ones, per class. The
questions are what this costs when the assumption is false, and why the method often classifies
well even then.

> The method

>> Bayes' theorem with independent predictors

Let \(x=(x_1,\dots,x_p)\) and let \(\pi_c\) be the prior probability of class \(c\). Under conditional
independence,

$$
P(c\mid x)=\frac{\pi_c\prod_{j=1}^pp_j(x_j\mid c)}{\sum_{c'}\pi_{c'}\prod_{j=1}^pp_j(x_j\mid c')},\qquad
\hat c(x)=\arg\max_c\Big[\log\pi_c+\sum_{j=1}^p\log p_j(x_j\mid c)\Big].
$$
\alttext(The posterior probability of class c given x is pi c times the product over predictors j of p j of x j given c, divided by the same expression summed over all classes. The predicted class maximises log pi c plus the sum over j of log p j of x j given c.)

Each predictor adds its own log-likelihood term, so the evidence from different predictors
simply adds up. `posterior_terms(newdata, row)` lists these terms for one row. The priors are
the class frequencies unless `NaiveBayesClassifierOptions::priors` sets them.

>> Numeric predictors: one Gaussian per class

A numeric predictor gets the density \(N(\mu_{cj},\sigma^2_{cj})\) in class \(c\), with the
maximum-likelihood estimates

$$
\hat\mu_{cj}=\frac1{n_c}\sum_{i:y_i=c}x_{ij},\qquad
\hat\sigma^2_{cj}=\frac1{n_c}\sum_{i:y_i=c}(x_{ij}-\hat\mu_{cj})^2+\epsilon,
$$
\alttext(mu hat c j is the mean of predictor j over the n c rows of class c. sigma hat squared c j is the mean squared deviation from it, plus a small epsilon.)

where \(\epsilon\) is `var_smoothing` (\(10^{-9}\)) times the largest overall predictor variance, a floor that
keeps a constant predictor from giving zero variance. Both conventions are scikit-learn's
`GaussianNB`. `gaussian_frame()` returns the estimates.

>> Categorical predictors: smoothed frequency tables

A categorical predictor with levels \(1,\dots,L_j\) gets a probability table per class,

$$
\hat P(x_j=\ell\mid c)=\frac{n_{c\ell}+\alpha}{n_c+\alpha L_j},
$$
\alttext(The estimated probability that predictor j takes level l in class c is the count n c l plus alpha, divided by n c plus alpha times the number of levels L j.)

where \(n_{c\ell}\) counts the class-\(c\) rows at level \(\ell\) and \(\alpha\) is `laplace_smoothing`. With \(\alpha=0\)
these are raw frequencies. A level never seen in a class then has probability zero, and a single
such observation vetoes the class whatever the other predictors say. \(\alpha=1\) (Laplace's rule
of succession) adds one imaginary observation per level. The predictor is one variable with
\(L_j\) levels, not \(L_j\) separate 0/1 dummy columns, which would not be independent of each other.
These are the conventions of scikit-learn's `CategoricalNB`. `categorical_frame()` returns the
tables.

>> The shape of the decision boundary

For two classes and Gaussian predictors the log posterior odds are

$$
\log\frac{P(1\mid x)}{P(0\mid x)}=\log\frac{\pi_1}{\pi_0}+\sum_j\Big[\log\frac{\sigma_{0j}}{\sigma_{1j}}
-\frac{(x_j-\mu_{1j})^2}{2\sigma^2_{1j}}+\frac{(x_j-\mu_{0j})^2}{2\sigma^2_{0j}}\Big],
$$
\alttext(The log posterior odds equal the log prior odds plus, for each predictor, the log ratio of the standard deviations minus the squared standardised distance to the class 1 mean over 2 plus the squared standardised distance to the class 0 mean over 2.)

a sum of one quadratic per predictor, with no cross terms \(x_jx_k\). The boundary is a quadric
whose axes are aligned with the predictors. If the two classes have equal variances for every
predictor, the squares cancel and the log odds are linear in \(x\). The model is then the same
as linear discriminant analysis with a diagonal covariance matrix. Linear discriminant analysis
estimates the full covariance and can tilt its boundary to follow correlations between
predictors; naive Bayes cannot.

>> What independence costs

Suppose predictor \(x_1\) is copied \(m\) times. The copies are perfectly dependent, but naive Bayes
counts each as new evidence, so the log-likelihood ratio of \(x_1\) is multiplied by \(m\) while the
prior term is not. The posterior probabilities become overconfident, and with unequal priors
the decision threshold moves too. Correlated, non-identical predictors give a weaker version
of the same double counting. The ranking of the classes can still be right when the
probabilities are badly wrong: classification only needs the sign of the log odds, not its
size. This is the explanation Domingos and Pazzani (1997) gave for naive Bayes' good accuracy
under violated assumptions.

>> Computation and exact leave-one-out

Products of many densities underflow, so everything is computed in logs. The posterior is
normalised with the log-sum-exp shift: subtract the largest log joint before exponentiating.

`training_accuracy()` is the resubstitution accuracy. For an honest estimate,
`loo_accuracy()` classifies every row by the model fitted without it. No refitting is needed:
removing row \(i\) of class \(c\) changes only \(n_c\), the class-\(c\) means, sums of squares and counts,
and the overall variances that set \(\epsilon\). Each is downdated in closed form, for example

$$
\hat\mu_{cj}^{(-i)}=\frac{n_c\hat\mu_{cj}-x_{ij}}{n_c-1},\qquad
SS_{cj}^{(-i)}=SS_{cj}-\frac{n_c}{n_c-1}\big(x_{ij}-\hat\mu_{cj}\big)^2,
$$
\alttext(The class mean without row i equals n c times the mean minus x i j, over n c minus 1. The sum of squares without row i equals the sum of squares minus n c over n c minus 1 times the squared deviation of x i j from the mean.)

so leave-one-out costs no more than resubstitution. The levels of a categorical predictor stay
those of the full data.

> Simple examples

>> Five rows by hand

One numeric predictor \(x\) and one categorical predictor \(g\), with classes *a* and *b*:

| \(x\) | \(g\) | class |
| ---: | :--- | :--- |
| 1 | u | a |
| 2 | u | a |
| 3 | v | a |
| 6 | v | b |
| 8 | v | b |

The priors are \(3/5\) and \(2/5\). Class *a* has \(\hat\mu=2\) and \(\hat\sigma^2=(1+0+1)/3=2/3\); class *b* has \(\hat\mu=7\) and
\(\hat\sigma^2=1\) (variance floor switched off). With \(\alpha=1\) and two levels, class *a* has
\(P(u)=(2+1)/(3+2)=0.6\) and \(P(v)=0.4\); class *b* has \(P(u)=(0+1)/(2+2)=0.25\) and \(P(v)=0.75\). For the query
\(x=4\), \(g=v\), the model adds one log term per predictor to the log prior.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  DF train;
  train.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{1, 2, 3, 6, 8});
  train.add_column("g", std::vector<std::string>{"u", "u", "v", "v", "v"});
  train.add_column("class", std::vector<std::string>{"a", "a", "a", "b", "b"});
  const dm::stats::NaiveBayesClassifier nb(train, "class ~ x + g", {.var_smoothing = 0.0});
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 4;
  std::print("{}\n\n{}\n\n", nb.gaussian_frame().to_md(md), nb.categorical_frame().to_md(md));

  DF query;
  query.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{4.0});
  query.add_column("g", std::vector<std::string>{"v"});
  std::print("Query x = 4, g = v:\n\n{}\n\n", nb.posterior_terms(query, 0).to_md(md));

  // The zero-frequency problem: class b never has g = u.
  DF strong_b;
  strong_b.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{4.5});
  strong_b.add_column("g", std::vector<std::string>{"u"});
  std::vector<double> alphas{0.0, 0.01, 0.1, 1.0, 10.0, 100.0}, p_b, p_u_b;
  for (const double a : alphas) {
    const dm::stats::NaiveBayesClassifier m(train, "class ~ x + g", {.laplace_smoothing = a, .var_smoothing = 0.0});
    p_b.push_back(m.predict_detail(strong_b).probability[0][1]);
    p_u_b.push_back(m.categorical_frame().double_at("probability", 2));
  }
  DF smoothing;
  smoothing.add_column("alpha", alphas);
  smoothing.add_column("P(g = u | b)", p_u_b);
  smoothing.add_column("P(b | x = 4.5, g = u)", p_b);
  std::print("Query x = 4.5, g = u:\n\n{}\n", smoothing.to_md(md));
}
```
