// result_end

\caption(Left: the fitted class densities of bill length times the priors (Adélie blue, Chinstrap red, Gentoo green), with each species' bill lengths as a strip below the axis. Right: the naive Bayes regions using bill length and depth only, with the training points and the leave-one-out errors (black centres). Tables: accuracies of four predictor sets; the Gaussian parameters and the island table of the full model; its leave-one-out confusion matrix; the within-species correlations of the bill measurements; and the log-posterior terms of one misclassified penguin.)
\alttext(Two panels. Left: three bell curves of bill length weighted by prior: a tall blue Adelie curve peaking near 39 mm, a green Gentoo curve near 47.5 mm and a lower red Chinstrap curve near 49 mm, overlapping heavily. Below the axis are three strips of points, one per species. Right: the bill length and depth plane split into three pale regions, Adelie on the left, Chinstrap upper right and Gentoo lower right, with the penguins drawn in their species' colours and about twenty misclassified ones marked with black centres along the region borders.)

*Accuracy.* The two bill measurements alone classify 93.3% of the penguins, with leave-one-out
accuracy equal to resubstitution. The four numeric measurements give 97.1%. Island and sex alone
give only 70.3%: island identifies Gentoo (Biscoe) and Chinstrap (Dream) penguins but cannot
separate Adélies on Biscoe or Dream from them. All six predictors give 98.2% resubstitution and
97.9% leave-one-out accuracy. For every model, LDA's leave-one-out accuracy is higher: 95.9%,
98.5% and 99.4%. scikit-learn (`GaussianNB` plus `CategoricalNB` with \(\alpha=1\) for the mixed model,
and `LinearDiscriminantAnalysis` with dummy-coded island and sex) agrees with every accuracy
here, including the leave-one-out ones, to the last digit.

*Parameters.* The Gaussian table shows which measurement separates which species. Bill length
separates Adélie (mean 38.8 mm) from the other two (47.6 and 48.8 mm). Bill depth, flipper length
and body mass separate Gentoo (15.0 mm, 217 mm, 5092 g) from the other two, which are close on
all three. Chinstrap and Adélie are therefore told apart mainly by bill length and island. The
island table shows Laplace smoothing at work. No Chinstrap was seen on Biscoe, but the estimated
probability is \((0+1)/(68+3)=0.0141\), not zero. A Chinstrap later seen on Biscoe can still be
classified correctly if its measurements are clear enough.

*Errors.* The leave-one-out confusion matrix has seven errors, all between Adélie and Chinstrap;
every Gentoo is classified correctly. The posterior terms show why row 38, a male Adélie from
Dream, is called a Chinstrap. The prior favours Adélie by 0.76 and body mass by 0.20. Island
favours Chinstrap by 0.95, because all 68 Chinstraps but only 55 of the 146 Adélies were on
Dream. Bill length favours Chinstrap by 0.73, flipper length by 0.33 and bill depth by 0.05. The sum puts Chinstrap ahead by
1.10, a posterior of 0.75. With independent terms the evidence is easy to read: here a long-billed
Adélie on the Chinstraps' island.

*Correlation.* Within each species, bill length and depth are positively correlated (0.39 for
Adélie, about 0.65 for the others): bigger penguins have bigger bills. Naive Bayes ignores this,
so its boundaries in the right panel are curves with no tilt along the correlation. LDA's pooled
covariance uses it. This is the situation of the correlated-predictor simulation, and the likely
source of LDA's advantage: with bills only, LDA misclassifies 14 penguins and naive Bayes 23.

> Conclusion

Naive Bayes replaces one joint model per class by a product of one-dimensional ones. It fits
any mix of numeric and categorical predictors in one pass, and its posterior splits into one
readable term per predictor. Its leave-one-out accuracy costs no more than its fit. When the
predictors are independent within each class it is the Bayes rule with estimated parameters, and
it converges to the Bayes error. Laplace smoothing is not optional for categorical predictors:
without it a single unseen level vetoes a class.

Its assumption fails in two ways. Redundant predictors are counted as independent evidence,
which leaves accuracy nearly intact but makes the probabilities overconfident: with eight copies
the log loss more than tripled. This is why naive Bayes often classifies well even when its
probabilities are poor (Domingos and Pazzani, 1997; Hand and Yu, 2001). Correlation that carries
information is a harder failure: naive Bayes cannot use the direction \(x_1-\rho x_2\), and its error
stays at that of \(x_1\) alone while LDA's falls to the Bayes error. On the penguins the cost is
small but visible, 97.9% against 99.4%, because bill length and depth are correlated within
species.

Use naive Bayes when there are many predictors relative to the data, when predictors are of
mixed types, or when per-predictor evidence is wanted. Ng and Jordan (2002) showed that it then
approaches its asymptotic error with far fewer samples than logistic regression. When the
numeric predictors are correlated and the data are enough to estimate a covariance matrix, LDA or
logistic regression will usually be more accurate. In either case, use the leave-one-out
accuracy, not the resubstitution accuracy, and treat the probabilities with caution.

> References

